\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Problem Statement}
\begin{keypoint}
The advent of quantum computing poses a significant threat to current cryptographic systems that safeguard financial transactions. Algorithms like RSA and ECC are vulnerable to quantum attacks through Shor's algorithm, creating an urgent need for quantum-resistant security measures.
\end{keypoint}

The rapid advancement of quantum computing technology presents a looming threat to the security infrastructure underpinning modern financial transactions \cite{quantum_threat}. Cryptographic algorithms such as RSA and Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC), which form the backbone of today's secure communication channels, face potential compromise through quantum algorithms like Shor's algorithm. This vulnerability creates an urgent need for quantum-resistant security measures to protect sensitive financial data.

Traditional approaches to securing payment gateways—including those employed by platforms like Razorpay \cite{razorpay}—may become obsolete in the post-quantum era. This research addresses this critical security challenge by integrating Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) with existing financial infrastructure, creating a hybrid system that leverages quantum principles to enhance transaction security.

\subsection{Objectives}
This research aims to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Develop and implement a quantum key distribution system based on the BB84 protocol for secure cryptographic key generation
    \item Integrate the QKD system with the Razorpay payment gateway API to secure financial transactions
    \item Implement and evaluate a neural network-based fraud detection system that complements quantum security measures
    \item Demonstrate the practical application of quantum cryptography in protecting payment data against potential quantum computing threats
    \item Evaluate the performance, security, and feasibility of quantum-secured payment processing compared to conventional methods
    \item Establish a framework for future quantum-safe financial systems
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Significance}
As quantum computing advances toward practical implementation, the security of financial systems becomes increasingly vulnerable. This research represents one of the first practical implementations of quantum cryptography in payment processing, providing valuable insights into both the challenges and opportunities of quantum-secure financial transactions.

The integration of QKD with Razorpay demonstrates a viable path for financial institutions to prepare for the quantum computing era, ensuring the continued security of digital transactions. Furthermore, our neural network fraud detection system provides an additional layer of security that works in concert with quantum-enhanced encryption, creating a multi-layered defense against both classical and quantum threats \cite{payment_security}.

\subsection{Quantum Key Distribution}
Quantum Key Distribution offers a method for two parties to establish a shared random secret key known only to them, which can then be used to encrypt and decrypt messages \cite{qkd_review}. Unlike conventional key exchange protocols that rely on computational complexity, QKD leverages the principles of quantum mechanics, specifically:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Heisenberg uncertainty principle:} The act of measuring a quantum system disturbs it, making it impossible to measure all properties of a quantum system simultaneously with arbitrary precision
    \item \textbf{No-cloning theorem:} It is impossible to create an identical copy of an arbitrary unknown quantum state
    \item \textbf{Quantum superposition:} Quantum bits can exist in multiple states simultaneously until measured
    \item \textbf{Quantum entanglement:} Quantum particles can be correlated in ways that have no classical analogue
\end{itemize}

These quantum mechanical properties allow QKD systems to detect any eavesdropping attempts during key distribution, providing information-theoretic security rather than relying on computational hardness assumptions.

\subsection{BB84 Protocol}

\begin{technote}
The BB84 protocol, proposed by Bennett and Brassard in 1984 \cite{bb84}, was the first quantum cryptography protocol. It uses quantum properties of photons to securely distribute cryptographic keys, detecting any eavesdropping attempts through quantum measurement disturbances.
\end{technote}

The BB84 protocol involves the following steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Qubit Preparation:} Alice prepares qubits in random states chosen from two non-orthogonal bases (typically the rectilinear and diagonal bases)
    \item \textbf{Quantum Transmission:} Alice transmits these qubits to Bob through a quantum channel
    \item \textbf{Measurement:} Bob measures each qubit in a randomly chosen basis (either rectilinear or diagonal)
    \item \textbf{Basis Reconciliation:} Alice and Bob publicly compare their basis choices over a classical channel, keeping only the measurements where they used the same basis
    \item \textbf{Error Estimation:} A subset of the matched bits is compared to detect eavesdropping (the presence of Eve)
    \item \textbf{Privacy Amplification:} Information-theoretically secure techniques are applied to the remaining bits to generate the final secure key
\end{enumerate}

Figure \ref{fig:bb84_protocol} illustrates the BB84 protocol operation. The security of this protocol stems from the quantum mechanical principle that measuring a quantum system in one basis disturbs measurements in a non-commuting basis, making eavesdropping detectable through increased error rates.

