\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Integration Challenges}
The integration of QKD with existing payment infrastructure presented several challenges:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Simulation vs. Real QKD Hardware}: Our implementation simulates QKD using Qiskit, but real-world deployment would require actual quantum hardware for key generation.
    
    \item \textbf{Key Distribution in Web Environment}: Securely distributing quantum-generated keys over the internet remains a significant challenge, as the current web infrastructure lacks quantum channels.
    
    \item \textbf{Performance Overhead}: The QKD process introduces additional latency compared to traditional key exchange, which could impact user experience in time-sensitive transactions.
    
    \item \textbf{Error Tolerance}: Finding the right balance between security (detecting eavesdropping) and usability (allowing some natural channel noise) required careful calibration of error thresholds.
    
    \item \textbf{Integration with Legacy Systems}: Payment gateways are designed to work with classical cryptography, requiring adaptation layers for quantum key integration.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Fraud Detection Insights}
The integration of AI-powered fraud detection provided several valuable insights:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Complementary Security Layers}: Fraud detection acts as a complementary layer to quantum-secure encryption, addressing different threat vectors.
    
    \item \textbf{Neural Network Advantages}: The new 4-layer neural network model demonstrated significant improvements over the previous approaches:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 5\% higher detection rate compared to the previous ML model
        \item Better handling of complex transaction patterns through deep learning
        \item Improved feature extraction capabilities through multi-layer processing
        \item Enhanced ability to detect sophisticated fraud patterns
        \item Only 2.1ms additional processing time compared to simpler models
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item \textbf{Feature Engineering Impact}: The neural network's ability to process derived features like amount-to-balance ratios and transaction timing significantly improved detection accuracy. One-hot encoding of categorical variables like payment methods enabled the model to detect payment-specific fraud patterns.
    
    \item \textbf{Model Selection Trade-offs}: Different fraud detection models offer varying balances between detection rate, false positives, and processing time. The heuristic model provides a lightweight option with minimal overhead, while the quantum-enhanced model offers the highest accuracy at the cost of increased processing time.
    
    \item \textbf{Sensitivity Calibration}: Finding the optimal sensitivity setting requires balancing security with user experience. Higher sensitivity settings increase detection rates but also false positives, which can lead to legitimate transaction rejections.
    
    \item \textbf{Real-time Performance Constraints}: Fraud detection must operate within strict timing constraints to maintain a seamless user experience, requiring optimized algorithms and efficient implementation.
    
    \item \textbf{Explainable AI Importance}: The ability to provide clear explanations for fraud detection decisions (risk factors) is crucial for user trust and regulatory compliance. The neural network's layer structure helps provide this explainability through its activation patterns.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{UI/UX Considerations}
The implementation of an Apple-inspired interface highlighted important UI/UX considerations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Balancing Aesthetics and Functionality}: Creating an elegant interface while maintaining clear communication of complex quantum concepts required careful design decisions.
    
    \item \textbf{Responsive Design Challenges}: Ensuring the interface worked well across device sizes required flexible layouts and adaptive components.
    
    \item \textbf{Performance Optimization}: Smooth animations and transitions required careful JavaScript optimization to maintain 60fps even during intensive simulation operations.
    
    \item \textbf{Accessibility Considerations}: The interface was designed to meet accessibility standards while maintaining the aesthetic quality, requiring careful color contrast selection and keyboard navigation support.
    
    \item \textbf{Feedback Mechanisms}: Clear visual feedback for each step of the quantum-secured transaction process was essential for user understanding and trust.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
This research has demonstrated the feasibility of integrating Quantum Key Distribution with the Razorpay payment gateway to enhance transaction security. We have implemented the BB84 protocol using Qiskit for quantum simulation and created a complete transaction flow that leverages quantum-generated keys for securing payment data. 

The addition of AI-powered fraud detection with our new 4-layer neural network model has significantly enhanced the system's ability to identify fraudulent transactions. The neural network architecture, with its 128-64-32-1 layer configuration and carefully selected activation functions, demonstrates superior detection capabilities compared to traditional heuristic approaches. Our experimental results show a 91% detection rate with only a 1.9% false positive rate, placing it close to the quantum-enhanced model in effectiveness while maintaining reasonable processing times.

The enhanced Apple-inspired user interface has further improved the system's usability, security, and visual appeal. Our experimental results show that while quantum-secured transactions introduce some performance overhead compared to classical approaches, the security benefits are substantial, especially in the context of emerging quantum computing threats. The multi-layered security approach combining quantum encryption with neural network-based fraud detection provides comprehensive protection against both quantum and classical threats.

The interface improvements demonstrate that quantum security technologies can be presented in an intuitive, visually appealing manner that enhances user experience while communicating complex security concepts effectively.

Future work will focus on optimizing the QKD process for reduced latency, exploring hardware implementations of quantum key distribution, enhancing the neural network model with additional layers and attention mechanisms, training with larger real-world datasets, and extending the approach to other financial and e-commerce platforms. The transition to quantum-secure financial systems with intuitive interfaces and intelligent neural network-based fraud detection represents a significant step forward in preparing for the post-quantum era of cybersecurity.

\section{Neural Network Fraud Detection Model}

\subsection{Model Architecture}
\begin{keypoint}
Our fraud detection system utilizes a 4-layer neural network (SimpleNN) optimized for transaction pattern recognition, incorporating ReLU and tanh activation functions with dropout regularization to prevent overfitting.
\end{keypoint}

The neural network architecture, illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:neural_network}, consists of:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Input Layer:} Accepts transaction features, including amount, balance, previous transaction history, transaction type (mapped from Razorpay payment methods), and other derived features
    \item \textbf{Hidden Layer 1:} 128 neurons with ReLU activation, followed by dropout (p=0.2)
    \item \textbf{Hidden Layer 2:} 64 neurons with tanh activation, followed by dropout (p=0.2)
    \item \textbf{Hidden Layer 3:} 32 neurons with ReLU activation, followed by dropout (p=0.2)
    \item \textbf{Output Layer:} Single neuron with sigmoid activation for binary classification (0 = legitimate, 1 = fraudulent)
\end{enumerate}

The model employs a combination of ReLU and tanh activation functions to capture both linear and non-linear patterns in transaction data. Dropout layers with a probability of 0.2 are used throughout the network to prevent overfitting and improve generalization to unseen data.

