\subsection{BB84 Protocol Implementation}
The BB84 protocol, introduced by Bennett and Brassard in 1984 \cite{bb84}, forms the foundation of our quantum key distribution approach. Our implementation follows these key steps:

\begin{figure}[!p]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
    % Alice's qubits
    \node[draw, circle, minimum size=0.7cm] (a1) at (0,0) {$|0\rangle$};
    \node[draw, circle, minimum size=0.7cm] (a2) at (0,-1.5) {$|1\rangle$};
    \node[draw, circle, minimum size=0.7cm] (a3) at (0,-3) {$|+\rangle$};
    \node[draw, circle, minimum size=0.7cm] (a4) at (0,-4.5) {$|-\rangle$};
    
    % Quantum channel
    \draw[thick, decorate, decoration={snake, amplitude=1mm, segment length=5mm}] (1.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
    \draw[thick, decorate, decoration={snake, amplitude=1mm, segment length=5mm}] (1.5,-1.5) -- (3.5,-1.5);
    \draw[thick, decorate, decoration={snake, amplitude=1mm, segment length=5mm}] (1.5,-3) -- (3.5,-3);
    \draw[thick, decorate, decoration={snake, amplitude=1mm, segment length=5mm}] (1.5,-4.5) -- (3.5,-4.5);
    
    % Bob's measurements
    \node[draw, rectangle, minimum width=1.2cm, minimum height=0.8cm] (b1) at (5,0) {$Z$};
    \node[draw, rectangle, minimum width=1.2cm, minimum height=0.8cm] (b2) at (5,-1.5) {$X$};
    \node[draw, rectangle, minimum width=1.2cm, minimum height=0.8cm] (b3) at (5,-3) {$Z$};
    \node[draw, rectangle, minimum width=1.2cm, minimum height=0.8cm] (b4) at (5,-4.5) {$X$};
    
    % Results
    \node[draw, diamond, minimum size=1cm] (r1) at (7,0) {0};
    \node[draw, diamond, minimum size=1cm] (r2) at (7,-1.5) {?};
    \node[draw, diamond, minimum size=1cm] (r3) at (7,-3) {?};
    \node[draw, diamond, minimum size=1cm] (r4) at (7,-4.5) {-};
    
    % Arrows
    \draw[->] (a1) -- (1.5,0);
    \draw[->] (a2) -- (1.5,-1.5);
    \draw[->] (a3) -- (1.5,-3);
    \draw[->] (a4) -- (1.5,-4.5);
    
    \draw[->] (3.5,0) -- (b1);
    \draw[->] (3.5,-1.5) -- (b2);
    \draw[->] (3.5,-3) -- (b3);
    \draw[->] (3.5,-4.5) -- (b4);
    
    \draw[->] (b1) -- (r1);
    \draw[->] (b2) -- (r2);
    \draw[->] (b3) -- (r3);
    \draw[->] (b4) -- (r4);
    
    % Labels
    \node at (0,1) {Alice's States};
    \node at (5,1) {Bob's Bases};
    \node at (7,1) {Measurement};
    
    % Key matching
    \draw[thick, blue] (0,0.5) -- (7,0.5);
    \draw[thick, blue] (0,-4) -- (7,-4);
    
    % Eavesdropper
    \node[draw, red, cloud, cloud puffs=10, minimum width=1.5cm, minimum height=1cm] (e) at (2.5,-2.25) {Eve};
    \draw[->, red, thick] (e) -- (2.5,-1);
    \draw[->, red, thick] (e) -- (2.5,-3.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
FloatBarrier
\caption{The BB84 Quantum Key Distribution Protocol: Alice prepares qubits in different states, which Bob measures in either the Z or X basis. When their bases match (rows 1 and 4), they obtain correlated results used for the secure key. An eavesdropper (Eve) disturbs the quantum state, revealing her presence.}
\label{fig:bb84_protocol}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Qubit Preparation:} Alice prepares qubits in various states, selecting randomly from the Z basis ($|0\rangle$, $|1\rangle$) or the X basis ($|+\rangle$, $|-\rangle$), as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:bb84_protocol}.

\subsection{Feature Engineering}

The model processes a rich set of features derived from transaction data:

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{|p{2.8cm}|p{7cm}|p{2.8cm}|}
\hline
\cellcolor{lightblue!30}\textbf{Feature Type} & \cellcolor{lightblue!30}\textbf{Description} & \cellcolor{lightblue!30}\textbf{Implementation} \\
\hline
Transaction Amount & Raw monetary value of the transaction & Min-Max scaled \\
\hline
Account Balances & Original and destination account balances & Min-Max scaled \\
\hline
Derived Features & \texttt{amount\_deducted} and \texttt{amount\_credited} calculations & Computed dynamically \\
\hline
Transaction Type & Mapped from Razorpay payment methods to PaySim transaction types & One-hot encoded \\
\hline
Previous Transactions & Count and total value of previous transactions & Exponentially weighted \\
\hline
Transaction Velocity & Rate of transactions over time & Time-windowed aggregation \\
\hline
Transaction Pattern & Sequence of transaction types and amounts & LSTM features \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Feature Set Used by the Neural Network Fraud Detection Model}
\label{table:features}
\end{table}

All numerical features undergo min-max scaling to ensure that the model treats each feature with appropriate weight. Categorical variables, such as transaction types, are one-hot encoded. The mapping from Razorpay payment methods to transaction types is shown in Table \ref{table:razorpay_mapping}.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\small
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|p{3.5cm}|p{3.5cm}|p{5.5cm}|}
\hline
\cellcolor{lightblue!30}\textbf{Razorpay Method} & \cellcolor{lightblue!30}\textbf{Mapped Type} & \cellcolor{lightblue!30}\textbf{Risk Profile} \\
\hline
Credit Card & PAYMENT & Medium-High: Sensitive to fraud \\
\hline
Debit Card & PAYMENT & Medium: Less risky than credit cards \\
\hline
UPI & CASH\_IN & Low-Medium: Fast settlement reduces risk \\
\hline
Net Banking & TRANSFER & Medium: Bank verification adds security \\
\hline
Wallet & CASH\_OUT & High: Less regulated than banking \\
\hline
FloatBarrier
EMI & DEBIT & Medium-High: Extended fraud window \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Mapping of Razorpay Payment Methods to Transaction Types}
\label{table:razorpay_mapping}
\end{table}

\subsection{Training Methodology}

The neural network is trained using the following methodology:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Loss Function:} Binary Cross-Entropy Loss with Logits (BCEWithLogitsLoss), which combines a sigmoid layer and binary cross-entropy loss for numerical stability
    \item \textbf{Optimizer:} Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and weight decay of 1e-5 for regularization
    \item \textbf{Batch Size:} 64 transactions per batch, balancing computational efficiency and gradient accuracy
    \item \textbf{Training Epochs:} 100 epochs with early stopping based on validation loss plateau
    \item \textbf{Data Split:} 70\% training, 15\% validation, 15\% testing
    \item \textbf{Class Balancing:} Weighted sampling to address the class imbalance between fraudulent and legitimate transactions \cite{fraud_nn}
    \item \textbf{Data Augmentation:} Synthetic minority oversampling for rare fraud patterns
\end{itemize}

The model is trained on a combination of PaySim synthetic data and real-world Razorpay transaction data, with sensitive information anonymized. In simulation mode, the system can generate synthetic training data when no pre-trained model or historical data is available.

